{
  "chapters": [
    {
      "chapter": 1,
      "title": "Arjuna Vishada Yoga",
      "verses": [
        {
          "reference": "1.1",
          "speaker": "Dhritarashtra",
          "text": "Dhritarashtra said: Having assembled on the holy plain in Kurukshetra desirous of fighting the battle, what did my people and the Pandavas do? O Sanjaya! (1.1)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "1.2",
          "speaker": "Sanjaya",
          "text": "Sanjaya said: On seeing the Pāndava army arrayed for battle, King Duryodhana went to his teacher and spoke these words: (1.2)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "1.3",
          "speaker": "Sanjaya",
          "text": "O teacher, behold the great army of the sons of Pāndu arrayed by your talented disciple, the son of Drupada. (1.3)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "1.4",
          "speaker": "Sanjaya",
          "text": "In that army are mighty archers and heroes, in battle equal to Bhima and Arjuna: Yuyudhāna, Virāta, and Drupada, each a mahāratha; Heroic Dhrishtaketu, Chekitāna, and the king of Kāśi; Purujit, Kuntibhoja, and Śaivya, all the best of men; Powerful Yudhāmanyu, brave Uttamaujā, Subhadrā’s son, and the sons of Draupadi— all mahārathas indeed. (1.4-1.6)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "1.7",
          "speaker": "Sanjaya",
          "text": "O best of the twice-born, let me also recount to you the leaders of my own army, those distinguished amongst ourselves. I shall name them, that you may know them all: (1.7)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "1.8",
          "speaker": "Sanjaya",
          "text": "Yourself and Bhishma and Karna; Kripa, who is ever victorious in war; Aśvatthāmā, Vikarna, Jayadratha, and Somadatta’s son; And many other heroes besides, armed with many weapons, each well skilled in battle, and all resolved to lay down their lives to serve my cause. (1.8-1.9)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "1.10",
          "speaker": "Sanjaya",
          "text": "But this army of ours, protected by Bhishma, is unlimited, and that army of theirs, protected by Bhima, is limited. (1.10)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "1.11",
          "speaker": "Sanjaya",
          "text": "Now take your proper places in front of your marshalled troops and protect Bhishma alone. (1.11)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "1.12",
          "speaker": "Sanjaya",
          "text": "Bhishma the grandsire, the glorious, the oldest of the Kurus, gave forth a lion-roar and blew his conch, causing joy to Duryodhana. (1.12)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "1.13",
          "speaker": "Sanjaya",
          "text": "Then conchs and kettle-drums, tabors and trumpets and cow-horns suddenly blared forth; and the sound was stupendous. (1.13)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "1.14",
          "speaker": "Sanjaya",
          "text": "Whereupon Mādhava and Pāndava, seated in their magnificent chariot yoked to white horses, also blew their celestial conchs. (1.14)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "1.15",
          "speaker": "Sanjaya",
          "text": "Hrishikeśa blew His conch, the Pānchajanya; Dhananjaya, the Devadatta; and Vrikodara, the doer of fearful deeds, blew his great conch, the Paundra. King Yudhishthira, the son of Kunti, blew his conch, the Anantavijaya; and Nakula and Sahadeva blew the Sughosha and the Manipushpaka. The great archer, the king of Kāśi; the great warrior Śikhandi; Dhristadyumna and Virāta; the unconquered Sātyaki; Drupada, and the sons of Draupadi, and the mighty son of Subhadrā, O Lord of the Earth, each blew his own conch. (1.15-1.18)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "1.19",
          "speaker": "Sanjaya",
          "text": "And that tumult, resounding through heaven and earth, rent the hearts of Dhritarāshtra’s followers. (1.19)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "1.20",
          "speaker": "Sanjaya",
          "text": "O King! Now seeing the Kauravas arrayed in battle order and the discharge of weapons about to begin, Arjuna took up his bow and spoke these words to Lord Krishna. (1.20)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "1.21",
          "speaker": "Arjuna",
          "text": "Arjuna said: O Achyuta, between the two armies draw up my chariot, that I may behold those who stand there eager to fight, and may know, on the eve of battle, with whom I must contend. (1.21-1.22)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "1.23",
          "speaker": "Arjuna",
          "text": "I would observe these warriors assembled here for the battle wishing to please the evil-minded Duryodhana. (1.23)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "1.24",
          "speaker": "Sanjaya",
          "text": "Sanjaya said: Advised thus by Arjuna Lord Krishna placed the noble chariot in the middle of the two armies, and in front of Bhishma and Drona, and all the Kings, and said: ‘O Arjuna! Behold those Kauravas gathered here.’ (1.24-1.25)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "1.26",
          "speaker": "Sanjaya",
          "text": "There Arjuna saw fathers, grand-fathers, teachers, uncles, brothers, sons, grandsons, companions, fathers-in-law, and friends in both the armies. (1.26)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "1.27",
          "speaker": "Sanjaya",
          "text": "Casting his eyes on all these kinsmen stationed on opposing sides, the son of Kunti was overcome with deep pity and sorrowfully spoke. (1.27)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "1.28",
          "speaker": "Arjuna",
          "text": "Arjuna said: O Krishna, at the sight of these my kinsmen, assembled here eager to give battle, my limbs fail and my mouth is parched. My body is shaken and my hair stands on end. The bow Gāndiva slips from my hand and my skin is on fire. I cannot hold myself steady; my mind seems to whirl. O Keśava, I see omens of evil. (1.28-1.30)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "1.31",
          "speaker": "Arjuna",
          "text": "O Krishna! I see many ill-omens and I do not understand what good could come by killing my own people in battle. (1.31)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "1.32",
          "speaker": "Arjuna",
          "text": "O Krishna! I do not desire victory or kingdom or pleasures. Of what avail are these kingdom, enjoyment, or even life itself to us. (1.32)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "1.33",
          "speaker": "Arjuna",
          "text": "Those for whose sake we desire kingdom, enjoyments, and pleasures—teachers, fathers, sons, grandfathers, maternal uncles, fathers-in law, brothers-in-law, grandsons and other relations are all standing here ready for battle, having abandoned their wealth and very life even. (1.33-1.34)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "1.35",
          "speaker": "Arjuna",
          "text": "These, O Madhusudana, I would not kill, though they should kill me, even for the sake of sovereignty over the three worlds— how much less for this earth! (1.35)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "1.36",
          "speaker": "Arjuna",
          "text": "O Janārdana, what joy can be ours in killing these sons of Dhritarāshtra? Sin alone will possess us if we kill these felons. (1.36)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "1.37",
          "speaker": "Arjuna",
          "text": "Therefore we ought not to kill our kinsmen, the sons of Dhritarāshtra; for, O Mādhava, how can we ever be happy by killing our own people? (1.37)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "1.38",
          "speaker": "Arjuna",
          "text": "Though they, their understanding overcome by greed, perceive no evil in the decay of families and no sin in hostility to friends, why, O Janārdana, should not we, who clearly perceive the evil in the decay of families, learn to refrain from this sin? (1.38-1.39)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "1.40",
          "speaker": "Arjuna",
          "text": "With the decay of a family, perish its dharmas, which have existed from time out of mind. With the ending of the dharmas, adharma overwhelms the whole family. When adharma overwhelms the family, O Krishna, the women of the family become corrupt; and when, O Krishna, the women are corrupt, there arises a mixing of castes. This mixture leads into hell the family itself as well as those who destroy it; for their ancestors fall, deprived of the offerings of rice-balls and water. (1.40-1.42)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "1.43",
          "speaker": "Arjuna",
          "text": "By these evil deeds of the destroyers of families, which result in the mixing of castes, the eternal dharmas of caste and family are uprooted. We have heard it said, O Janārdana, that inevitably the men whose family dharmas are destroyed dwell in hell. (1.43-1.44)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "1.45",
          "speaker": "Arjuna",
          "text": "Alas, we are resolved to commit a great sin, in that we are ready to slay our kinsmen to satisfy our greed for the pleasure of a kingdom! (1.45)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "1.46",
          "speaker": "Arjuna",
          "text": "Far better would it be for me if the sons of Dhritarāshtra, weapons in hand, should slay me in the battle, unarmed and unresisting. (1.46)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "1.47",
          "speaker": "Sanjaya",
          "text": "Sanjaya said: Arjuna, having spoken thus on the battlefield, cast aside his bow and arrow and sank down on his chariot-seat, his mind overcome with grief. (1.47)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "chapter": 2,
      "title": "Sankhya Yoga",
      "verses": [
        {
          "reference": "2.1",
          "speaker": "Sanjaya",
          "text": "In this manner, when Arjuna was plunged in a state of despondency, with eyes blurred with tears, Lord Krishna addressed this speech to him. (2.1)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.2",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "The Blessed Lord said:O Arjuna! Whence, in this critical situation, has this mental dejection takes hold of you, shameful and Heaven-excluding? (2.2)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.3",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "O Arjuna! Do not yield to impotence. It does not befit you. Cast off this wretched weakness of heart. Arise, O scorcher of enemies! (2.3)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.4",
          "speaker": "Arjuna",
          "text": "Arjuna Said:O Krishna! Bhishma and Drona are my teachers fit to be worshipped. How can I attack them with arrows in the battle? (2.4)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.5",
          "speaker": "Arjuna",
          "text": "It would be better, indeed, to live on alms in this world rather than to slay these high-souled teachers. But if I kill them, even here I shall enjoy wealth and desires stained with their blood. (2.5)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.6",
          "speaker": "Arjuna",
          "text": "We do not know which would be the better for us: that we should conquer them or they should conquer us. Arrayed against us stand the very sons of Dhritarāshtra, after slaying whom we should not wish to live. (2.6)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.7",
          "speaker": "Arjuna",
          "text": "(O Krishna!) My mind is distracted by the taint of pity and I am ignorant of what my duty is, and so, I ask you to teach me what is good for me. I am your disciple. I take refuge in you. (2.7)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.8",
          "speaker": "Arjuna",
          "text": "I can find no means to drive away this grief which is drying up my senses. I will not be able to dispel it even if I win a prosperous, unrivaled kingdom on earth with sovereignty like the demigods in heaven. (2.8)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.9",
          "speaker": "Sanjaya",
          "text": "Sanjaya Said:Having spoke thus, Arjuna, the destroyer of foes, finally said to Lord Krishna, “I will not fight” and became silent. (2.9)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.10",
          "speaker": "Sanjaya",
          "text": "O king Dhritarashtra! Seeing Arjuna lamenting in the middle of two armies, Lord Krishna, as if laughing loud, spoke these words. (2.10)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.11",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "The Blessed Lord said:O Arjuna! You grieve for those for whom there need be no sorrow, yet you speak words of wisdom. The wise do not grieve for the dead or living. (2.11)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.12",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "Never was there a time when I did not exist, nor you, nor these kings of men. Never will there be a time hereafter when any of us shall cease to be. (2.12)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.13",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "Just as the man in this body passes through the various stages of boyhood, youth, and old age, like so, he passes into another body after death. The wise know it and are not deluded. (2.13)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.14",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "Contact of the senses with the objects produces heat and cold, pain and pleasure. These experiences come and go, and are impermanent. Endure them, O Arjuna! (2.14)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.15",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "The firm man who is not affected by pain and pleasure, who remains equal-minded, surely is fit for immortality, O Arjuna, Chief of mortals! (2.15)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.16",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "The unreal has no being, the real has no non-being. The final truth of these two has been seen indeed by those who have experienced the essence of things. (2.16)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.17",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "(O Arjuna!) Know thatAtmaby which the whole universe is pervaded is indestructible. No one can cause the destruction of the imperishable. (2.17)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.18",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "These bodies which belong to the real imperishable, unknowable Atma are said to be subject to an end; therefore fight, O Arjuna! (2.18)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.19",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "He who thinks thatAtmaslays, or he who thinks thatAtmais Slain, both these do not know the Truth.Atmadoes not slay, nor is slain. (2.19)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.20",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "Atma is not born, nor does it ever die; after having been it does not cease to be, unborn, eternal, changeless and ancient, it is not killed when the body is destroyed. (2.20)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.21",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "O Arjuna! He who knows thatAtmais birthless, deathless, real, and imperishable, whom can he slay or cause to be slain? (2.21)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.22",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "Just as a man cast off his worn-out clothes and puts on new ones so also the Self throws away its worn-out bodies and takes other fresh bodies. (2.22)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.23",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "Weapons cannot cut, fire cannot burn, water cannot wet, wind cannot dry up thisAtma. (2.23)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.24",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "The Atma cannot be cut, cannot be burnt, cannot be wetted, and also cannot be dried up. It is eternal, all-pervading, firm, immovable, and ancient. (2.24)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.25",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "This self is unknowable by the senses, unthinkable by the mind, and is not subject to any kind of change. Knowing this, you should not grieve. (2.25)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.26",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "O mighty-armed Arjuna! Even if you think that Self is constantly born and dies, you should not grieve in this way. (2.26)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.27",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "For to that which is born, death is certain, and to that which is dead, birth is certain. Therefore you should not grieve over the unavoidable. (2.27)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.28",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "All beings are unmanifest in their beginning, O Bhārata, manifest in their middle state, and unmanifest again in their end. Why, then, lament for them? (2.28)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.29",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "Some look on the Self as a wonder; some speak of It as a wonder; some hear of It as a wonder; still others, though hearing, do not understand It at all. (2.29)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.30",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "O Arjuna! The Self existing in the body of all beings is never slain. So it is not right for you to grieve for any creature. (2.30)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.31",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "And moreover, considering your own duty, you should not waver, for, there is nothing higher for a Kshatriya than a righteous war.(2.31)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.32",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "Happy indeed are the kshatriyas, O Pārtha, to whom comes such a war, offering itself unsought, opening the gate to heaven.. (2.32)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.33",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "But if you do not fight this righteous war you would incur sin by abandoning your own duty, name, and fame. (2.33)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.34",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "And also, these people will speak of your everlasting dishonor, and to one who is honored, dishonor is worse than death. (2.34)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.35",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "Moreover, those great warriors from whom you received honor formerly would think that you turned away from battle out of fear, and regard you with little respect hereafter. (2.35)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.36",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "Your enemies belittle your ability, and in various ways speak words of ill-fame and shame about you. What is there more painful than this? (2.36)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.37",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "If you are killed in the battle, you will go to heaven; if you win, you will enjoy the earth. Therefore arise, O son of Kunti, resolved to fight. (2.37)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.38",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "Having an equal mind in pain and pleasure, gain and loss, victory and defeat, engage in battle and thereby you will not incur sin. (2.38)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.39",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "O Arjuna! So far the knowledge of Sankhya Yoga about Atma is declared. Now hear the knowledge of Karma Yoga, knowing which you shall cast off the bondage of work. (2.39)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.40",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "In this, no effort is ever lost and no harm is ever done. Even very little of this dharma saves a man from the Great Fear. (2.40)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.41",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "O Arjuna! There is only one faith and thought for those who practice this Karma Yoga. The minds of others are divided into various branches, and their thoughts and speculations are endless.  (2.41)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.42",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "O Arjuna! The unwise utter flowery speech, taking pleasure in the laudatory words of the Vedas, and say that there is nothing else but pleasures and enjoyments either here or in Heaven. They are full of desire, with heaven as their highest goal, leading to new births as the effect of their own Karma, and they engage themselves in a multiplicity of specific works for the purpose of acquiring enjoyments and prosperity. The mind of such men who are drawn away by attachment to pleasure and wealth, cannot be concentrated to remain fixed in the ecstasy of divine contemplation. (2.42-2.44)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.45",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "The first part of the Vedas (Karma Kanda) deal with material things pertaining to the three Gunas. One should transcend the three Gunas, become free from the operation of the pairs of opposites, take refuge in the pure sattvic state, and firmly establish himself in Atma.(2.45)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.46",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "To an enlightened person who has known the Self, all the Vedas are of as much use as is a reservoir of water in a place where there is a flood. (2.46)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.47",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "You have the right to work only, and not to the fruits of work. Let not the fruit of action be your motive, nor let your attachment be to inaction. (2.47)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.48",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "O Arjuna! Steadfast in Yoga, giving up all attachment, unmindful of success or failure, do your work. Such equanimity of mind is called Yoga. (2.48)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.49",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "O Arjuna! Work with attachment is far inferior to nishkama karma. Therefore seek refuge in desireless action with equanimity of mind. Those who work for fruits and rewards are wrecked.(2.49)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.50",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "The man of equanimity born of wisdom releases himself both from good and bad even in this life. Therefore strive for nishkama karma with an equal mind. Yoga is skill in action. (2.50)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.51",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "Wise men endowed with equanimity, having abandoned the fruits of action, go to the abode beyond all sorrow and evil. (2.51)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.52",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "When your mind has crossed the mire of delusion, you will achieve indifference regarding things already heard and things yet to be heard. (2.52)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.53",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "When your intellect which is perplexed by hearing the various sastras becomes steady and immovable in ecstatic concentration, then you shall attain union with the Supreme Being.(2.53)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.54",
          "speaker": "Arjuna",
          "text": "Arjuna said:O Krishna! What are the characteristics of a man of steady wisdom? How does the man merged in the super-conscious state, sit and move? (2.54)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.55",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "The Blessed Lord said:When a man renounces completely all the desires of the mind, and when he is fully satisfied with his mind fixed in Atma, then he is declared to be a man of steady wisdom. (2.55)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.56",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "He whose mind is not troubled in sorrow, who does not hanker after pleasures and is free from attachment fear and hatred, is called the sage of steady wisdom. (2.56)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.57",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "He who has no attachment to anything anywhere, who does not rejoice and hate when good and bad things happen, his wisdom is fixed and steady. (2.57)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.58",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "When the yogi, like the tortoise drawing back its limbs into its own shell, withdraws all the senses from the sense objects, his wisdom is firmly fixed. (2.58)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.59",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "When a man rejects the sense objects by withdrawing the senses, he becomes free from the sense world only. The longing or taste for them still remains in the mind. Even this longing is removed when the self is perceived. (2.59)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.60",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "O Arjuna! The turbulent senses carry away the mind even of the learned man though he is striving to control them.(2.60)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.61",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "Having restrained all the senses the harmonized should sit intent on me. His wisdom is steady whose senses are under control. (2.61)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.62",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "As a man contemplates sense-objects, attachment for them arises, from attachment, desire for them will be born, from desire arises anger, from anger comes delusion, from delusion, comes loss of memory, from loss of memory, comes destruction of discrimination, and from destruction of discrimination he perishes.(2.62 & 2.63)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.64",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "But the self-controlled man free from attraction and repulsion, with his senses under restraint though moving among objects, attains peace. (2.64)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.65",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
          "text": "When a man attains peace, all sorrow and suffering caused by the unbalanced mind and rebellious senses come to an end. By peace and purity, the mind is soon fixed in the Self. (2.65)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "2.66",
          "speaker": "Krishna",
